# Frappe Development Patterns

## ORM Usage
- Use `frappe.get_doc()`, `frappe.get_all()`, `frappe.db.get_value()` for standard queries
- Raw SQL is fine for joins, aggregations, and complex filters — use parameterized queries (`%(param)s`, never f-strings with user input)
- `frappe.get_all()` silently ignores non-existent fields — always verify field names against DocType JSON

## API Endpoints
- `@frappe.whitelist()` for all exposed APIs
- `allow_guest=True` only for webhooks and public widget endpoints
- First line of every endpoint: access check (`_check_excom_access()` or equivalent)
- Use `frappe.throw()` for user-facing validation errors
- Use `frappe.log_error()` for background failures (never `print()`)

## Background Work
- `frappe.enqueue()` for background jobs — never spawn threads
- `frappe.db.commit()` after mutations in non-request contexts
- `frappe.publish_realtime("event", data, user=user)` for pushing updates to frontend

## DocTypes
- All new DocTypes prefixed with "Excom" to avoid namespace conflicts
- Never modify ERPNext/Frappe core DocTypes — use Custom Fields or child tables
- Run `bench migrate` after any DocType JSON change
- Heavy business logic goes in `services/` layer, not in DocType controllers

## Error Handling
- Never bare `except:` — catch specific exceptions
- `frappe.throw(_("message"), frappe.PermissionError)` for permission failures
- For webhook responses needing specific HTTP codes, use `werkzeug.wrappers.Response` directly

## Secrets
- Secrets in `site_config.json` via `frappe.conf`
- OAuth tokens use Frappe's Password field type (encrypted at rest)
- Never log full message content — log message IDs only
